  {"clause_id": "c001", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The opening sentence has no operative verb — \"This Liquidating Trust Agreement ..., by and among [parties], executed in connection with the Chapter 11 Plan ..., which provides for the establishment of a liquidating trust\" is a fragment that never states what the parties do. A court asked what this paragraph itself effects would have to supply the missing predicate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.18},
    {"issue": "All capitalized terms not defined here take their meaning from the Plan \"as the same may be amended,\" a document not attached, so the content of terms such as \"Effective Date\" and \"Reserved Claims\" can shift after execution. Read with Section 9.8, which makes the Plan control over this instrument, the definitional referent is indeterminate.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This recital states the Trust is created \"pursuant to, and to effectuate, the Plan\" and could be argued to control the operative scope of the Trustee's authority in Sections 3.2 and 3.5, which are stated to be exercisable \"except as set forth in this Trust Agreement or the Plan.\" A party could invoke the recital to narrow operative powers to those the Plan expressly confers.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.10}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The recital says the Trust is created \"on behalf of, and for the benefit of, the holders of allowed Class 2 claims\" only, which cannot be squared with Section 3.6, which directs the Trustee to make distributions to holders of Class 1 claims from the proceeds of the assets securing those claims. A court would have to decide whether the recital's exclusive statement of beneficiaries controls the operative distribution provision.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The recital fixes the \"sole purpose\" as liquidation for the benefit of allowed Class 2 claim holders, which conflicts with Section 3.6's mandate that the Trustee also distribute to holders of Class 1 claims and with Section 1.1(b)'s assumption of unpaid Plan obligations. A court would have to decide whether the recital's \"sole purpose\" limits those operative provisions.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.26}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c006", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The \"Register\" is defined and its maintenance is required only in this recital; no operative provision obliges the Trustee to create or maintain it, yet Sections 2.1, 4.7(a), 6.2 and 9.6 all depend on it. A court would have to decide whether a recital can be the source of that operative duty.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007", "issues": [
    {"issue": "\"[E]ach and every asset of the estate\" is not defined or itemized, leaving open whether it reaches causes of action that belong to individual noteholders rather than to the estate, and whether it captures claims not yet identified at the Effective Date. The scope of the transferred \"Trust Assets\" is the term on which the Trustee's standing to sue turns.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.35},
    {"issue": "The transfer is stated to be \"free and clear of any lien, claim or interest in such property of any other person or entity,\" which cannot be squared with Section 3.6, which presupposes surviving liens by directing distributions to Class 1 claim holders \"from the proceeds of sales or other disposition of the assets securing said claims.\" It is also in tension with Section 1.1(b), which takes the same assets subject to listed liabilities.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This subsection transfers the Trust Assets \"subject to the following liabilities,\" which contradicts Section 1.1(a)'s transfer of the same assets \"free and clear of any lien, claim or interest.\" A court would have to reconcile the two sentences of the same section.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This subsection says the Trustee \"shall be entitled to transfer to the Trust any and all assets of the Debtor,\" although Section 1.1(a) already effects the transfer of every estate asset automatically upon the Effective Date. Whether any asset remains to be transferred, and in whose name, cannot be answered from either provision alone.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.18}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The section directs that Trust Assets be treated for tax purposes by \"the holders of allowed Class 3 claims,\" while every other reference in the same sentence and in the rest of the instrument (Recitals, Sections 1.1, 2.1, 3.6) is to Class 2. The reference to Class 3 is on its face a scrivener's error that changes who is bound by the tax treatment.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.42}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c012", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The mandatory custodian requirement is defined only by an open-ended illustration — \"a custodian (such as a qualified bank or a company that is a member of a national securities exchange)\" — with no definition of \"qualified\" and no stated class into which other custodians must fall. Whether a given depository satisfies this sentence cannot be determined from the words used.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.20}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c013", "issues": []},
  {"clause_id": "c014", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This section fixes the record holders as \"the holders of allowed Class 2 claims,\" but Section 2.2 permits Liquidating Trust Interests to be transferred on written notice, after which the record holder is no longer a Class 2 claimant. Section 3.6's direction to distribute \"to holders of Class 2 claims\" then cannot be squared with the Register-based definition.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c015", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The general prohibition on \"any steps, directly or indirectly, to facilitate the development of a secondary market\" sits against four enumerated specifics, all of which are public-market activities, leaving unclear whether ordinary transfer assistance falls inside the ban. Whether the list confines the general words or merely illustrates them is a live general-versus-specific question.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c016", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This section grants the Trustee \"exclusive authority over administration of the Liquidating Trust,\" which cannot be squared with Section 3.3(e), under which the Trustee is supervised by, reports to, and must obtain the approval of the Committee for dispositions of assets worth $5,000 or more. A court would have to decide which of the two governs administration.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c017", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The duty to act \"in an expeditious but orderly manner\" and \"not unduly prolong the duration of the Liquidating Trust\" states two standards that pull against each other with no benchmark or deadline to measure either. Nothing in the section says how the tension between speed and orderliness is resolved.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.16}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c018", "issues": [
