  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c020", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The flat ban on commingling Trust Assets with \"the property of any other person\" sits uneasily with Section 1.4, which permits registration of Trust Assets in the name of a nominee and requires use of a custodian, and with Section 4.6, which permits demand deposits at any bank the Trustee deems appropriate. Whether pooled custodial or deposit arrangements breach this subsection is not answerable from it alone.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.12}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c021", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This subsection forbids any action that would subject the Trust to registration under the Investment Company Act of 1940, while Section 6.1 expressly contemplates that the Trustee may determine the Trust \"is required to comply with the registration and reporting or other requirements of ... the Investment Company Act of 1940\" and must then act to comply. The two provisions cannot both be given effect if registration becomes required.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c022", "issues": [
    {"issue": "This subsection limits the Trustee's compensation and expense reimbursement \"to the extent, and in the manner provided in, the Plan,\" which cannot be squared with Section 4.7(a), which fixes hourly rates and lets the Trustee raise them on ten days' notice without Bankruptcy Court approval. Which of the two sets the compensation ceiling would have to be construed.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
  ]},
  {"clause_id": "c023", "issues": [
    {"issue": "The Committee-approval requirement for any disposition of an asset with gross fair market value of $5,000 or more is immediately followed by a carve-out permitting \"any other action with respect to such assets ... without obtaining approval of the Committee,\" and \"any other action\" is not bounded. The exception is capable of being read to swallow the rule — for example a settlement or compromise of a claim worth far more than $5,000.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.33}
